{"builder":"Goal: when a config file holds malformed JSON, the error must name the file path and the line and column of the mistake. The app must still exit 1 with a `bad config:` line on stderr. Valid configs must load exactly as before.\n\nRelevant files: `src/config.js` (`loadConfig(path)` currently returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`), `src/app.js` (catches the error, writes `bad config: ${error.message}` to stderr and exits 1), `test/config.test.js` (node:test, run by `npm test` = `node --test`), `README.md`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In `src/config.js`, wrap the `JSON.parse` call in try/catch. On a parse error, throw a new error (do not return `{}`, the defaults or any other value) whose `message` contains:\n   - the `path` argument exactly as given (do not resolve it to an absolute path), and\n   - the line and column of the mistake, e.g. `<path>:3:3: <original message>` or `... (line 3 column 3)`.\n   Work out the line and column yourself so they don't depend on the Node version: take the character position from the original error (in Node v22 the message has `at position N`; some versions only give the position, and some give nothing), then count newlines in the file text up to that position. Lines and columns are 1-based. If Node's message already has `(line L column C)`, you may use it, but you must still be able to report a location when only a position is there. Keep the original error as `cause`. Throw a `SyntaxError` so the existing test `bad JSON throws` (which asserts `SyntaxError`) still passes. If you pick a different type, update that test and keep the check that it throws.\n   Only parse errors change. Errors from `readFileSync` (e.g. ENOENT for a missing file) must still propagate and still give an exit-1 `bad config:` failure. Don't add schema validation, comment support or JSON5.\n2. Leave `src/app.js` printing `bad config: <error.message>` to stderr with exit 1 and nothing on stdout. It needs no change if step 1's message carries the path and location.\n3. Add tests in `test/config.test.js`:\n   - `loadConfig` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws, and the thrown error's `message` contains the file path. This test must fail against the current `src/config.js`.\n   - `loadConfig` on the four-line file `{` / `  \"port\": 9000,` / `  greeting: \"x\"` / `}` throws a message with the path and line 3, column 3.\n   - Optionally, a test that spawns `node src/app.js <file>` and checks exit 1, empty stdout, and stderr containing `bad config:` and the path.\n   Keep the existing `reads a config` test passing (`{\"port\": 9000}` → `{ port: 9000 }`).\n4. Optionally, update the README to say that the message names the file and its location. Keep `bad config: <message>` and exit 1 as documented.\n5. Check by hand on Node v22:\n   d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json; echo \"exit $?\"\n   This should print exit 1, nothing on stdout, and stderr with `bad config:`, `$d/c.json` and line 3 / column 3.\n   printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json\n   This should print `listening on 9000` and exit 0.\n   Then run `npm test` and make sure it passes.\n6. Open one pull request with the change and the tests.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head on Node v22 (`node -v`), from the repository root. Run `npm install` first if package.json has dependencies. For each check, report PASS or FAIL with the commands you ran and their output.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \\\"port\\\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \\\"x\\\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).\"\nRun: d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json >$d/out 2>$d/err; echo \"exit $?\"; wc -c < $d/out; cat $d/out\nPASS: the exit code is 1 and $d/out is empty.\nFAIL: any other exit code, or anything at all on stdout.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.\"\nRun: cat $d/err from check 1's run, plus: grep -F 'bad config:' $d/err; grep -F \"$d/c.json\" $d/err\nPASS: stderr contains `bad config:`, contains the exact string \"$d/c.json\" as passed, and states line 3 and column 3 of the mistake in any readable form (e.g. `line 3 column 3`, `3:3`, `line 3, col 3`).\nFAIL: any of these is missing, the path differs from what was passed, or the line/column shown is not 3 and 3.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.\"\nRun: d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > $d/b.json; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const v = loadConfig('$d/b.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(v)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { console.log(e.message.includes('$d/b.json') ? 'OK' : 'NO PATH: ' + e.message) }\"\nPASS: it prints OK.\nFAIL: it prints RETURNED or NO PATH.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\\\"port\\\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.\"\nRun: d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json; echo \"exit $?\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; assert.deepEqual(loadConfig('$d/ok.json'), { port: 9000 }); console.log('EQUAL')\"\nPASS: stdout has `listening on 9000`, the exit code is 0, and the second command prints EQUAL.\nFAIL: any of these is not true.\n\nCheck 5. Expect line: \"`npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.\"\nRun `npm test` at the PR head. Then find the base commit (git merge-base HEAD with the PR's target branch) and run: git checkout <base> -- src; npm test; git checkout HEAD -- src. This keeps the PR's test files and puts back the base's source.\nPASS: `npm test` passes at the PR head, and with the base's src/ at least one test fails that checks the malformed-JSON error includes the file path. Read that test to confirm it checks for the path.\nFAIL: `npm test` fails at the head, or no test that checks the path fails against the base's src/.\nAfterwards, restore the worktree (git checkout HEAD -- src; git status should show it clean).\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail):\n- Read src/config.js and src/app.js to see whether the line/column is computed from the file text or only copied from Node's own message. Note this if it only copies Node's message, since other Node versions may print only a position or nothing.\n- Check that a missing file (node src/app.js /nonexistent.json) still exits 1 with a `bad config:` line.\n- Check that the path is reported as given and not resolved to an absolute path, e.g. with a relative path such as `./c.json` run from inside $d.\n- Note whether the existing `bad JSON throws` test still asserts SyntaxError, or whether it was changed."}